  "Provided Approach": "Start by identifying the sensors, then trace the neural pathway, and finally explain the effector responses and feedback outcome. 1) Arterial baroreceptors in the carotid sinus and aortic arch sense stretch in the vessel wall. 2) An increase in blood pressure increases stretch and baroreceptor firing. 3) Afferent signals travel via the glossopharyngeal nerve from the carotid sinus and via the vagus nerve from the aortic arch to the nucleus tractus solitarius in the medulla. 4) The medulla integrates this information: higher pressure increases parasympathetic activity to the heart and decreases sympathetic outflow. 5) This reduces heart rate, cardiac contractility, and peripheral resistance, lowering blood pressure. 6) Conversely, lower blood pressure reduces baroreceptor firing, increases sympathetic outflow, and decreases parasympathetic activity, raising heart rate, contractility, and vasoconstriction to increase blood pressure. 7) The result is a negative-feedback loop that resists acute changes in arterial pressure."
}

