{
  "Learning Path Planning": "A 12-week speaking-focused plan aligned with Lily's interests, learning style, and Mandarin development. Phase 1, Weeks 1 to 2, Speaking Foundations: Begin with a short baseline speaking recording using a nature or art prompt. Focus on high-frequency vocabulary from familiar stories, simple sentence patterns, and basic tone awareness. Include daily 5 to 10 minute tone drills using visual tone cards, gestures, and shadowing. Phase 2, Weeks 3 to 6, Storytelling Practice: Use picture books, illustrated scenes, and short oral retellings. Lily will practice describing art images, narrating simple stories, and acting out roles with a teacher or partner. Each week, she will record one short speaking sample and compare it with a previous sample to track progress. Phase 3, Weeks 7 to 10, Fluency Building: Increase speaking time through guided conversations, nature walk interviews, storytelling circles, and role play. Emphasize smooth pronunciation, tone accuracy, pacing, and confidence. Use a simple checklist for clarity, tone, fluency, and participation. Phase 4, Weeks 11 to 12, Confidence Performance: Lily creates a short illustrated storybook or nature-themed comic and presents it in two to three minutes. The final project includes a self-assessment, teacher feedback, and a celebration of growth. Progress will be measured through speaking recordings, tone accuracy checks, participation, and a simple fluency rubric.",
  "Personalized Recommendations": "Use Lily's strengths in reading comprehension and vocabulary by turning written texts into spoken activities such as retelling, summarizing, and predicting. Because she is visual and kinesthetic, provide color-coded tone cards, tone mapping, gesture cues, picture prompts, art-based speaking tasks, and nature scavenger hunts. To improve pronunciation and tone accuracy, use short daily drills with minimal pairs, slow speech modeling, shadowing, mirror work, and gentle feedback on mouth position and pitch. To build speaking fluency and confidence, start with low-pressure speaking opportunities such as paired practice, small group storytelling, recorded journal entries, and role play with familiar characters. Connect learning to her interests by asking her to draw scenes, make a story map, describe plants or animals, or interview a nature guide in Mandarin. Use her bilingual background carefully as a support, for example comparing English and Mandarin vocabulary or using translation only when it helps. Celebrate effort and improvement, provide positive and specific feedback, and track progress with a simple goal chart that includes tone accuracy, speaking time, participation, and confidence."
}

