  "Personalized Recommendations": "Use visual supports such as arrays, ten-frames, number lines, and color-coded factor cards; use kinesthetic activities like jump-rope skip counting, sports relay fact drills, and manipulatives such as counters or stones; connect math to sports and nature by scoring points, tracking stats, and counting plants, rocks, or leaves; leverage strengths by starting each lesson with addition/subtraction problem solving and gradually adding multiplication/division steps; provide short 10-15 minute focused practice blocks with frequent movement breaks; use positive feedback and small rewards for completing fact families; monitor accuracy on multiplication/division fact families and word problems weekly; for errors, prompt Alex to draw a picture or make an array before solving; include parent practice ideas such as snack sharing, team practice counts, and nature walks with multiplication tasks."
}

