  "Personalized Recommendations": "1. Use visual study tools such as diagrams, flowcharts, and color-coded note templates to improve note-taking. 2. Complete a short environmental science experiment each week, such as testing water quality, modeling ecosystems, or designing a pollution-reduction system, to connect learning with experimentation. 3. Create a 'Visual Learning Board' for each unit with key concepts, vocabulary, images, and connections to real-world environmental issues. 4. Practice note-taking before, during, and after class using the Cornell method or a graphic organizer. 5. Reduce test anxiety by using a consistent pre-test routine: organized notes, one 10-minute review session, deep breathing, and positive self-talk. 6. Use timed practice quizzes in a low-pressure setting to build confidence and familiarity with test formats. 7. Maintain a moderate study schedule with focused 25-35 minute sessions and short breaks to avoid burnout. 8. Track progress with a simple visual checklist showing completed experiments, improved note-taking, and reduced test-anxiety strategies used."
}

