{
  "Learning Path Planning": "Phase 1 (Weeks 1-4): Control Systems Foundations. Study signals and systems, Laplace transforms, transfer functions, stability, PID tuning, and system identification. Use MATLAB/Simulink to visualize step responses, Bode plots, and root locus plots. Deliverable: build a virtual or physical motor speed/position control project in Python or MATLAB. Phase 2 (Weeks 5-8): Theoretical AI and Machine Learning. Review linear algebra, calculus, probability, optimization, and core algorithms such as linear/logistic regression, decision trees, SVMs, random forests, and neural networks. Use visual Python notebooks, MATLAB, and diagrams to connect math to code. Deliverable: reproduce classic ML results on a robotics dataset, such as gripper failure prediction or object classification. Phase 3 (Weeks 9-12): Robotics and Automation Integration. Learn robot kinematics, dynamics, motion planning, sensor fusion, perception, and basic reinforcement learning. Use ROS, Gazebo, PyTorch, and MATLAB/Simulink for simulation and hardware-in-the-loop. Deliverable: design a simulated robotic cell or automated material handling system with a vision-based inspection task. Phase 4 (Weeks 13-16): Capstone and Portfolio. Combine control theory, ML, and robotics in a manufacturing automation project, such as predictive maintenance for a robotic arm, adaptive PID using reinforcement learning, or an autonomous mobile robot for warehouse logistics. Present results with diagrams, dashboards, code repositories, and a short technical report.",
  "Personalized Recommendations": "Use hands-on projects as the main way to learn: pair every theory topic with a simulation or physical experiment. Start with a microcontroller-based control project, such as controlling a DC motor, servo arm, or simple robotic gripper, and gradually add sensors, computer vision, and ML models. Build visual learning aids: block diagrams, state-space plots, Bode plots, neural network architecture diagrams, and annotated Jupyter notebooks. Strengthen control systems by solving worked examples in MATLAB/Simulink, tuning PID controllers on real or simulated hardware, and studying system identification with data from manufacturing equipment. Strengthen AI theory by deriving major algorithms step by step, visualizing loss functions, gradient descent, and decision boundaries, and comparing algorithms using benchmark datasets. Use Python and MATLAB together: MATLAB/Simulink for control simulation, Python for data science, ML, and robotics integration. Follow a project-based curriculum: robotics projects should include perception, control, and optimization, such as object grasping, path planning, or production line anomaly detection. Keep a portfolio of code, visualizations, and project write-ups to document progress. Consider studying classic resources such as control systems textbooks, linear algebra and probability primers, Andrew Ng or CS229 machine learning materials, PyTorch or scikit-learn tutorials, ROS tutorials, and robotics capstone projects. Aim for a balanced weekly plan: 1 hour of theory, 2 hours of simulation or hands-on implementation, and 1 hour of review, testing, and documentation."
}

