{
  "Learning Path Planning": "1. Foundation phase (4-6 weeks): Establish a focused core of modernist literature and abstract expressionism by selecting 3-5 key texts and 10-15 major artworks. Create a visual-reflective research notebook combining annotations, images, timelines, and short interpretive responses to build conceptual fluency without overextending reading scope. 2. Synthesis phase (6-8 weeks): Develop a connective framework for literature-visual art intersections using themes such as fragmentation, consciousness, form, color, gesture, and abstraction. Use comparative charts and concept maps to organize large volumes of research into manageable, publishable themes. 3. Research and writing phase (8-10 weeks): Narrow the series into 2-3 paper topics that are publishable and aligned with the student’s strengths in critical analysis, creative writing, and visual interpretation. Draft one paper fully before expanding to others, using iterative cycles of outline, draft, visual analysis, and revision. 4. Publication preparation phase (ongoing): Polish the strongest essay first, seek feedback from interdisciplinary faculty or writing communities, and identify suitable journals or publications for literature, art, and interdisciplinary studies. Establish a recurring weekly routine to maintain steady progress while protecting time for reflection and creative work.",
